Base-model prompting (0-shot and few-shot) converges to identical variant-level behavior regardless of strategy: both models classify all unresolved variants correctly and zero resolved variants, collapsing to an always-predict-unresolved baseline (45.83\%, below majority 54.17\%). Per-test accuracy varies widely (46--72\%) but this variation is superficial; variant-level evaluation exposes complete degradation (Appendix~\ref{app:few_shot}).

\paragraph{Formulation divergence.} At repository-level complexity under our 4--8B LoRA protocol, \cls{} and \cwm{} diverge sharply on Qwen3: the gap is 25.59\pp{} at 4B (\cls{} 83.63\% vs.\ \cwm{} 58.04\%, seed 42; Figure~\ref{fig:scaling_gap}) and 19.31\pp{} at 8B (84.48\% vs.\ 65.17\%). Both gaps are an order of magnitude larger than at function level (1--3\pp{}, Table~\ref{tab:scaling}); formulation effectiveness is complexity-dependent. \cwm{} falls below the majority baseline at both scales ($-$14.92\pp{} at 4B, $-$7.79\pp{} at 8B): negative utility for this model/formulation combination.

\paragraph{Cross-model validation.} DeepSeek-Coder is consistent with a formulation-driven explanation, though single-seed constraints limit causal attribution: both families show \cls{} $>$ \cwm{}, with function$\to$repo degradation of ${\sim}$5/22\pp{} (\cls{}/\cwm{}, Qwen3-8B) and 4--12/25--30\pp{} (DeepSeek-6.7B, \cls{} seed-dependent). The generation bottleneck is consistent across families. DeepSeek \cls{} degradation ranges from ${\sim}$4\pp{} (s42, drop preprocessing) to ${\sim}$12\pp{} (s123, truncate preprocessing), reflecting both preprocessing and checkpoint selection differences (Table~\ref{tab:taxonomy}).

\paragraph{Repo-level failure mode taxonomy.} Variant-level analysis ($n{=}48$, Appendix Table~\ref{tab:variant_level}) reveals three distinct failure modes (Table~\ref{tab:taxonomy}). DeepSeek exhibits both modes depending on training configuration: decision collapse under truncate preprocessing (s123, fail recall ${\sim}$14\%), aggregation penalty under drop preprocessing (s42, fail recall ${\sim}$51\%, variant-level 81.25\%). Decision collapse is configuration-dependent, not model-inherent. \traj{} avoids aggregation penalty by predicting variant outcomes directly (Qwen3: 72.92\% at both 4B and 8B; DeepSeek: 75.35\% $\pm$ 5.00, 6 seeds); Qwen3 data now permits within-model comparison. \cls{} achieves the highest per-test accuracy across configurations, but variant-level performance is configuration-dependent; failure mode is a function of formulation $\times$ model $\times$ configuration $\times$ complexity.

